The '''economy of Sarta''' is a high-income, service-led mixed economy with a substantial specialised manufacturing sector. Sarta's economic development was shaped first by agriculture and regional trade, then by coal extraction and state-directed industrialisation, and finally by liberalisation, privatisation, and the expansion of internationally traded services. The country reached high-income status in 2018. The figures in the infobox are 2024 estimates unless otherwise noted; national accounts are reported in Sartan dollars, with selected comparisons given in [[International dollar|International dollars]] (ID).

Sarta has an area of approximately 252,170 km² and a population of 41,243,191 in 2021. Its average national population density is approximately 164 people per km², although settlement is uneven. The largest concentrations are found around Mariapolis, Bahia Alta, and Jibravista, while the Sierra Madre and other upland districts are more lightly populated.

The Sartan economy remains closely connected to the wider Adonian economic order. It uses the Sartan dollar ($S), a national currency introduced after the [[1976–1981 global economic crisis]] and maintained under a loose peg to the [[International dollar]] ($). Sarta is a member of the [[Adonian Community]]. The post-crisis currency reform gave the government a domestic monetary instrument while preserving a stable external reference for trade, reserves, and financial contracts. The economy is relatively diversified compared with the coal- and manufacturing-based system of the nineteenth and early twentieth centuries, but it remains exposed to foreign demand, imported energy, and changes in regional investment.

== Economic history ==

=== Extraction and royal companies ===

Before industrialisation, Sarta's economy was based primarily on agriculture, local commerce, and regional trade. The return of the coastal territories under the [[Occidental Agreement of 1846]] created the conditions for a larger export economy. Coal deposits in the [[Sierra Madre]] were developed to supply Sartan industry and the markets of [[Volisania]].

The crown established the [[Compañía Real de Minaria de Sarta]] in 1847 to coordinate coal extraction, transport, and export. It was part of a wider system of royal companies that managed strategic sectors and public works. This model concentrated capital and infrastructure in state-linked institutions, while also contributing to labour conflict and political pressure for parliamentary reform.

Coal supported the expansion of railways, ports, workshops, and urban industry. The extraction economy declined gradually in the twentieth century as coal imports fell and newer energy sources became more important. Mining continued in selected districts, but it ceased to be the central engine of national growth.

=== State-led industrialisation ===

During the first half of the twentieth century, Sarta expanded into metalworking, transport equipment, ship repair, chemicals, and military supply. The authoritarian governments of the period used state direction and foreign capital to accelerate industrial development, particularly through their relationship with [[Volisania]]. This created a larger industrial workforce and more urban employment, but it also left Sarta dependent on external markets and political protection.

After the collapse of the wartime regime in 1947, the state retained an important role in reconstruction and industrial planning. The postwar government invested in transport, education, public services, and manufacturing while integrating Sarta into the institutions of the Adonian Community. Manufacturing relocated from Volisania to Sarta during the 1960s and 1970s, partly because of lower labour costs and access to ports.

The [[1976–1981 global economic crisis]] exposed the limits of this model. The Camboriú War and the resulting energy shock reduced manufacturing demand, raised transport costs, and weakened the international dollar system. Sarta's central bank withdrew from the International dollar scheme during the crisis and began issuing the Sartan dollar as a national currency. The change gave the government greater control over domestic liquidity and exchange-rate policy while introducing currency risk and uncertainty for foreign trade. Sarta later restored a formal exchange-rate relationship with the International dollar in 1994, establishing the loose peg that continues in modified form. Sarta's government initially refused to privatise major industries and attempted to maintain them through the downturn. The policy protected public ownership but prolonged stagnation and unemployment.

=== Liberalisation and the service transition ===

A conservative government elected in 1984 introduced broad privatisation and encouraged private investment. State enterprises in transport, communications, finance, utilities, and manufacturing were sold or reorganised. The policy was controversial in industrial districts, where public companies had been major employers, but it brought new capital into sectors that had suffered from underinvestment.

The reforms accelerated the transition toward services. Banking, insurance, logistics, telecommunications, tourism, retail, business administration, and information services expanded, while manufacturing became more specialised and less labour-intensive. Sarta experienced rapid economic growth between 1990 and 1994, including several years of double-digit GDP growth, before unemployment rose again after 1995.

The Labor Party's return to government in 1998 did not reverse privatisation. Instead, Labor governments combined private-sector growth with public investment in education, healthcare, retraining, housing, and regional infrastructure. Conservative governments generally emphasised fiscal restraint, investment, and competitiveness. This alternation created disagreement over the distribution of growth without restoring the former state-led industrial model.

Sarta remained an upper-middle-income economy through most of the 1998–2021 period. Rising productivity, a larger skilled workforce, higher-value service exports, and deeper integration with the Adonian Community allowed it to enter the high-income category in 2018.

== Structure of the economy ==

=== Services ===

<!-- Image prompt: Photorealistic documentary-style view of a modern Sartan financial and business-services district, glass and stone office buildings, pedestrians, transit, street-level commerce, subtle national character, no text, no watermark. -->

Services account for approximately 68% of Sarta's gross domestic product and are the largest source of employment. The sector includes finance, insurance, logistics, telecommunications, tourism, education, healthcare, retail, public administration, and business-processing services.

The highest-productivity service activities are concentrated in Mariapolis and the principal coastal cities. Financial services and corporate administration are centred in the national capital, while logistics and port services are more important in Bahia Alta and other western Aethiopean ports. Tourism, education, and healthcare employ workers throughout the urban network.

Sarta's service economy developed in stages. The first stage involved the expansion of transport, commerce, and public administration. The second followed privatisation in the 1980s and included banking, telecommunications, and private logistics. The third involved information services, specialised business processing, higher education, and other activities requiring a skilled workforce.

=== Manufacturing ===

<!-- Image prompt: Photorealistic documentary-style interior of a modern Sartan specialised manufacturing plant, workers assembling precision machinery and electrical equipment, clean industrial floor, realistic safety equipment, no text, no watermark. -->

Manufacturing contributes approximately 19% of GDP. It is more important to exports and productivity than to total employment, since many factories have become increasingly mechanised. Major activities include industrial machinery, electrical components, transport equipment, chemicals, processed food, construction materials, marine equipment, and specialised assembly.

The sector developed from the coal, metalworking, and ship-repair industries of the nineteenth and early twentieth centuries. Manufacturing relocated from Volisania during the 1960s and 1970s, and some of those firms later remained in Sarta after shifting toward more technical and specialised production.

Industrial activity is concentrated around Mariapolis, Bahia Alta, Jibravista, and the older northern industrial districts. Former coal regions retain engineering firms, technical schools, repair facilities, and smaller manufacturers even where extraction itself has declined.

=== Agriculture, forestry and fishing ===

Agriculture, forestry, and fishing account for approximately 4% of GDP but occupy a much larger share of the country's land. Mechanisation and urbanisation have reduced agricultural employment, while commercial farms have become more specialised and productive.

Sartan agriculture supplies domestic markets with cereals, fruit, vegetables, livestock, dairy products, timber, and specialty crops. Food processing connects rural production to the manufacturing sector. Fishing remains important in coastal communities and supports both domestic consumption and processed-food exports.

The main agricultural regions are the river valleys, coastal plains, and lower slopes outside the Sierra Madre. Upland regions are more suitable for forestry, livestock, mining remnants, and tourism than for intensive agriculture.

=== Construction ===

Construction contributes approximately 6% of GDP. It expanded during postwar reconstruction, urban growth, industrial relocation, and the development of transport infrastructure. In the modern economy, construction is closely tied to housing, commercial development, ports, roads, railways, telecommunications infrastructure, and tourism.

Construction employment is cyclical. It tends to grow during periods of private investment and public infrastructure spending, but falls quickly when interest rates rise or foreign demand weakens.

=== Mining and energy ===

Mining and energy extraction account for approximately 3% of GDP. Coal was historically the most important mineral resource, but declining coal demand and changing energy systems reduced its role during the twentieth century. Remaining extraction includes coal, construction materials, and selected metal and mineral deposits.

Sarta imports a substantial share of its petroleum and natural gas. This dependence contributed to the severity of the 1976–1981 crisis and encouraged later investment in energy efficiency, hydroelectricity, wind power, and solar generation. Domestic energy policy remains concerned with the cost of imported fuel and the reliability of industrial supply.

== Trade and economic integration ==

Sarta is an export-oriented economy. Its principal exports include machinery, electrical equipment, transport components, processed food, chemicals, specialised manufactured goods, financial services, tourism, and logistics. The country imports petroleum, natural gas, electronics, pharmaceuticals, industrial components, advanced machinery, and some food products.

[[Volisania]] has historically been Sarta's most important economic partner. The two countries share extensive trade, investment, and labour links. The 1994 freedom-of-movement agreement for labour allowed Sartan workers to seek employment in Volisania and created a significant flow of remittances to Sartan households.

[[Jacan]] is another major regional market and transport partner. Sarta's trade with Jacan includes manufactured goods, food products, energy, transport services, and financial activity. Membership in the [[Adonian Community]] provides access to common economic institutions, financial standards, and regional investment networks.

Relations with [[Guajicutea]] were restored after the Second World War. The relationship developed through shipping, tourism, education, migration, and commercial exchange. The post-colonial relationship is generally framed as an equal regional partnership rather than an imperial connection.

== Labour market and migration ==

Sarta's labour market reflects the transition from coal and heavy industry to services and specialised manufacturing. Employment in finance, communications, education, healthcare, logistics, tourism, and business administration has expanded, while extraction and mass industrial employment have declined.

The 1994 labour freedom-of-movement agreement with Volisania became an important pressure valve during periods of unemployment. Sartan workers moved to Volisanian cities for industrial, construction, service, and professional employment. Remittances supported households in Sarta and helped sustain smaller towns, although long-term emigration also contributed to concerns about the loss of skilled workers. Kastruneto, Echia, and Mercia also provide important markets for Sartan manufactured goods, professional services, and investment, while Burawa and the United Midlands are significant sources of energy, machinery, and other imported goods.

Labour politics remain divided between demands for wage growth and employment protection and demands for flexibility and international competitiveness. Labor governments have generally expanded retraining and social programmes, while conservative governments have emphasised private investment and lower barriers to hiring.

== Regional economies ==

Sarta's economic geography is uneven. Mariapolis is the country's principal metropolitan, administrative, financial, and cultural centre. Its economy is dominated by government, finance, corporate services, education, retail, and high-value professional employment.

Bahia Alta and Occidental Province retain the strongest maritime and industrial orientation. Their economies include ports, logistics, ship repair, manufacturing, warehousing, tourism, and international trade. Jibravista is a major inland industrial and transport centre linked to agricultural distribution and river corridors.

Cinepolis and the northern districts of Fuertelon retain the legacy of coal extraction and early industrialisation. Their modern economies rely more on engineering, technical education, repair, specialised manufacturing, and heritage tourism than on mining itself.

The Sierra Madre and other upland areas are more lightly populated. Forestry, agriculture, hydroelectricity, tourism, and limited mineral extraction are more important there than finance or high-density services. River valleys connect these areas to the larger cities and ports.

== Income and inequality ==

Sarta's high-income classification reflects national average income and productivity rather than equal prosperity across all regions or occupations. Wages are highest in finance, information services, medicine, advanced manufacturing, education, and corporate administration. Tourism, retail, logistics, care work, and some forms of public-facing service employment generally pay less.

Regional inequality also follows the economic transition. Mariapolis and the major ports benefited most from services, foreign investment, and specialised manufacturing. Former coal districts and rural areas have depended more heavily on public infrastructure, regional development programmes, agriculture, remittances, and transfers.

Housing costs have risen in the largest metropolitan areas, particularly where high-value services and international investment are concentrated. The political conflict between Labor and conservative governments has therefore focused increasingly on wages, housing, taxation, public services, and regional redistribution rather than on whether Sarta should remain a market economy.

== Public finance and economic policy ==

Sarta's public sector provides education, healthcare, transport infrastructure, social insurance, and regional development. The state no longer directly controls most productive industry, but it remains a major purchaser, regulator, and infrastructure investor.

The Sartan dollar was created during the restructuring of the financial system in the [[1976–1981 global economic crisis]]. Sarta's central bank withdrew from the International dollar scheme and replaced direct use of the currency with a national issue. Sarta did not permanently abandon monetary integration: in 1994, the central bank restored an exchange-rate relationship with the International dollar. The resulting loose peg allows the Sartan dollar to move within a policy band rather than maintaining a permanently fixed conversion rate. This gives the [[AC Central Bank]] limited room to respond to domestic conditions while preserving confidence in cross-border contracts and trade.

The arrangement supports financial stability, but it does not provide unlimited monetary independence. Large adjustments to the peg can raise import prices, worsen external debt burdens, or damage confidence. Sartan authorities therefore use interest rates, reserve management, fiscal policy, wages, productivity, and structural reforms alongside exchange-rate intervention. The currency system reflects a compromise between national monetary control and continued integration with the Adonian financial order.

Economic policy since 1998 has balanced liberalisation with social protection. Labor governments have generally used public expenditure to support employment and reduce regional inequality. Conservative governments have prioritised fiscal stability, private investment, and competitiveness. The alternation between the two has produced changes in emphasis without fundamentally changing the mixed, service-led structure of the economy.

